---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { COMMUNITY_URL, OBSIDIAN_INSTALL_LINK, PLUGIN_ID, PLUGIN_RELEASES, PLUGIN_REPO, SITE_NAME } from "../lib/site";
---

<Layout title={`Install · ${SITE_NAME}`} description="Install Codazo from Obsidian's community plugins, configure a provider profile, and what to expect from local models.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Install</span>
        <h2>From Obsidian's community plugins.</h2>
        <p>Codazo is listed in Obsidian's community plugin directory. Obsidian desktop 1.11.5 or newer is required; mobile is not supported yet.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <ol>
          <li>In Obsidian, open <strong>Settings › Community plugins</strong>. If this is your first community plugin, turn off Restricted mode.</li>
          <li>Choose <strong>Browse</strong>, search for <strong>Codazo</strong>, and select <strong>Install</strong>. Or open <a href={OBSIDIAN_INSTALL_LINK}>Codazo directly in Obsidian</a>.</li>
          <li>Select <strong>Enable</strong>, then open <strong>Settings › Codazo</strong> to add a provider profile.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The <a href={COMMUNITY_URL} rel="noreferrer">directory page</a> shows the current version. Prefer to install by hand? Each <a href={PLUGIN_RELEASES} rel="noreferrer">GitHub release</a> carries <code>main.js</code>, <code>manifest.json</code>, and <code>styles.css</code> with SHA-256 checksums; put them in <code>.obsidian/plugins/{PLUGIN_ID}/</code>.
          The <a href={PLUGIN_REPO} rel="noreferrer">repository</a> holds the readable source and the same unminified <code>main.js</code>, so you can build it yourself with <code>npm ci &amp;&amp; npm run build</code>.
        </p>

        <h2>Configure a provider profile</h2>
        <p>Open <strong>Settings › Codazo</strong> and create a profile. You can keep several and pick the active one.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Endpoint</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>OpenAI</td><td><code>https://api.openai.com/v1</code></td><td>Fixed. Works with <code>json_object</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ollama Cloud</td><td><code>https://ollama.com/v1</code></td><td>Fixed. Hosted inference, not local. Use the exact model tag.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Personalizado</td><td>any OpenAI-compatible base URL</td><td>OpenRouter, a local server, and so on. HTTPS, or plain HTTP only to <code>localhost</code>, a <code>.local</code> name, or a private-network address.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          The key is sent only to that profile's destination, never to Codazo. Store it for the session, or on this device through Obsidian's Secret Storage; there is no plaintext fallback.
          Changing a profile's destination asks for a new key. Deleting a profile deletes its key.
        </p>

        <h2>Local models</h2>
        <p>A local server such as LM Studio works with a custom profile, with three settings that matter:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Formato de respuesta:</strong> <code>json_schema</code>. Local servers usually reject <code>json_object</code>, and instructions alone let small models drift off the contract.</li>
          <li><strong>Campo de límite de tokens:</strong> <code>max_tokens</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Tiempo máximo de espera:</strong> raise it; a local model may take a minute or two.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Prefer models that answer directly. Reasoning models spend their whole budget thinking and often never produce the JSON inside the timeout.
          In a check against LM Studio, a small non-reasoning model satisfied the contract in <code>json_schema</code> mode; a 30-billion-parameter reasoning model never answered.
          Any model, local or hosted, can still break the contract; when it does, the notice names the field or rule it broke.
        </p>

        <h2>Your Codazo folder</h2>
        <p>Saved notes go under the folder set in settings, <code>Codazo</code> by default:</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Subfolder</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>Reviews</code></td><td>Saved reviews, with the exact source and a reloadable payload.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Study</code></td><td>Study sheets, also reloadable.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Extractos</code></td><td>Excerpts of text you are reading, always saved.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Revisions</code></td><td>Your own revision notes, created on request.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Palabras</code> and <code>Verbos</code></td><td>One note per word and per verb-and-tense, with a <code>codazo-progreso</code> property.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Exports</code></td><td>JSON and HTML exports.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Files are never overwritten; a name collision gets a new name, and word and verb notes that already exist are left alone.</p>

        <h2>Other settings</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Interface language:</strong> Spanish (default) or English, for the plugin's own text only.</li>
          <li><strong>Pane sections start open:</strong> summary, observations, words, and verbs are collapsible; choose whether they begin open or closed.</li>
          <li><strong>Save reviews automatically</strong> and <strong>Create word and verb notes automatically:</strong> both off by default.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
